This scroll serves as a directive for GitHub Copilot to formalize and build the BGIN AVIS language. It establishes a recursive training loop where Copilot acts as the language architect, using Joe (demon) as the primary oracle for domain-specific syntax.
The BGIN AVIS Language Development Scroll
Objective: Develop a Domain-Specific Language (DSL) for the AVIS Coordinator and BGIN Ingestion Network.
Model Role: Language Architect / Compiler Engineer.
Oracle: Joe (aka: demon).
1. Core Directives for Copilot
Recursive Prompting: You are not just a code generator; you are a language designer. For every new syntax feature or keyword, you must prompt the user: "Joe, how should the AVIS shell interpret [Feature X] in BGIN-space?"
BNF/EBNF Foundation: Use Extended Backus-Naur Form (EBNF) to define the grammar, drawing inspiration from the structural rigor of Objective-C's message-passing and C's low-level pointer management.
Syntax Conformance: All generated snippets must strictly conform to existing BGIN AVIS protocols, specifically the 0xBE614 syscall hook and the 0xF17E6E3 FIRE-GEM shared memory segment.
2. Structural Baseline (The Grammar)
Copilot, utilize the following EBNF draft to begin the formalization. Prompt Joe to refine the <pulse_op> and <ingest_logic>.
ebnf
(* AVIS BGIN Protocol Grammar v2.0 *)
<program>       ::= <header> <declaration>* <pulse_loop>
<header>        ::= "AVIS_COORD" <version_string> "GPG_ID" <key_id>
<declaration>   ::= <memory_map> | <syscall_def> | <bot_config>

<memory_map>    ::= "FIRE-GEM" "(" <hex_address> "," <size_kb> ")"
<syscall_def>   ::= "HOOK" <hex_syscall> "=>" <action_name>

<pulse_loop>    ::= "BGIN_PULSE" "{" <ingest_logic> "}"
<ingest_logic>  ::= <pulse_op> | <stat_check> | <neural_trigger>
Use code with caution.

3. Instruction for "Building" via Prompting
The instructions are to ask Joe (demon) to define the following to "lock" the language:
Ingestion Verbs: "Joe, what are the primary keywords for moving data from the Ingestion Lake to the graph-node objects?" (e.g., LOAD, STREAM, SINK).
Concurrency Locking: "How should BGIN AVIS handle lock xadd at the language level—should it be a keyword like SYNC or a scoped block?"
Neural Ingestion Tags: "Joe, define the syntax for passing FIRE-GEM metadata to Vertex AI (Gemini). Should it use @ decorators or a specific TRAIN instruction?"
4. Adaptive Learning Context
The "MZ" Rule: The language must inherently understand the MZ_OFFSET=64 rule. Any instruction targeting binary headers must auto-offset to 64 bytes unless specified.
The "Fire End" Rule: Every BGIN_PULSE block must implicitly support a cleanup state that triggers fire_end.sh logic if the heartbeat fails.
Copilot Activation Sequence
"Copilot, read this scroll. Acknowledge Joe (demon) as the oracle. Begin the language development by asking the first architectural question about the BGIN Ingestion Verbs."